[majwr@cc.flinders.edu.au (J.W.Rice) writes:]
[] Can someone tell me what system shocks are, what causes them, and how to
[] prevent them?

simple answer:
    your polymorph ring attempted to polymorph you.  you didn't survive
the traumatic shock to your system and so you died.  you can prevent the
system shock with polymorph control (ring) or you can safely survive
the hazardous trauma by having enough hit points (you have to figure
out how many ;)

long winded answer: (umm, order out for pizza now ...)
    NetHack has a rich diverse background/culture that it draws upon.
this is, in part, what makes it super.  the downside is that NetHack
doesn't _meticulously_ bring its users into the culture, rather it
assumes that the users know these things or that they can somehow pick
things up.  to a large extent that reasoning is correct (for observant
players) but things could be better.

   system shock is a term borrowed, i believe, from D&D.  the nuances
of language are such that in the dungeon this simple term leads to
some unfortunate confusion.  it is great to have D&D-style polymorph
stuff in NetHack but the wording should change, i.e. system shock
becoming the more direct polymorph trauma (or failure/mishap/etc.).
ambiguity (albeit inadvertant) serves no purpose here.

    with that in mind, consider how the (observant) player reads
NetHack messages and makes associations.  shock is generally related
to electricity and with an inspired leap you could possibly
understand why your "health currently feels amplified" after eating
certain monsters.  unfortunately, other interpretations are possible
and shock is also used in polymorph terms.  oh well ...  how many
players have resorted to shock resistance to prevent this system
shock?  that this happens is a plain and simple fact.  that it should
happen is arguable (and i would just say no!).  with the simplest
of tweaks, the game could steer the player in the right direction.

    consider words like "shudder" which, in my opinion, should be
associated as tightly as possible with polymorph and nothing else.
the simple word "seems" should be tightly bound to things which are
temporarily unkosher.  if NetHack was more meticulous along these
lines (if that is humanly possible) it would be a slight improvement
in what is, without a doubt, the best adventure game going.

    would this eventually lead to the subtle but certain undoing of
the fine fascination that NetHack has over its players?  methinks not.
talk it over ...

